    #[tool(
        description = "List the machines an org/team sees, as the Machines panel lists them: This Mac first, then the team's shared machines, then your private ones. Required: `orgSlug`. Optional: `team` (include that team's shared machines; describe_setup lists orgs and teams). Returns `[{machineId, kind, label, scope, declaration, binding, canPublish, status, attention, capabilities, remoteMcpControl?, terminalServer?, load1?, cpuCount?, memUsedBytes?, memTotalBytes?, diskFreeBytes?, diskTotalBytes?, diskPath?, sampledAt?, machineStatsSupported?}]`. `machineId` is what open_workspace {machineId} and open_remote_tab take (`local` = This Mac). `kind`: `local` | `ssh`. `scope`: null for This Mac, `{kind:\"personal\"}`, or `{kind:\"team\", orgSlug, team}`. `declaration` (null for This Mac): the shareable half — `{host, port, jumpHost, defaultBaseDir, pinnedHostKeyFp, pinnedServerId}`; `binding`: your own connection settings for it, null when you never connected. `status.state`: `connected` (with `workspaces`, the open workspace count), `idle`, `connecting`, `handshaking`, `reconnecting` (`attempt`), `disconnected`, `unreachable` (`lastReachedAtMs`, `retryInSecs`), `connectionFailed` (`lastError`), `needsCredential`, `authFailed` (`reason`), `hostKeyChanged`, `notInstalled` (its terminal server is not installed), `versionMismatch`, `terminalServerFrozen`. `attention`: how many agents there wait on you. `capabilities.agentCallbacks`: whether agents launched there can reach this app's MCP (inbox, roles, progress, hooks): true only for a set-up private-account machine with remote MCP control on (set_machine_remote_mcp_control); a team-shared machine has none. `terminalServer` (connected machines only; absent otherwise): the running terminal server next to this app — `{serverVersion?` (the app release it was built for; absent for an older build that predates build stamps), `appVersion`, `upToDate`, `relation` (`same` | `older` | `newer` | `otherBuild`: This Mac's server belongs to another CodeSense build sharing this Mac — `upToDate` is false, but only `older` servers are out of date and offered the update), `announcedVersion`, `buildCommit?`, `builtAtMs?`, `buildDigest?`, `devBuild`, and for This Mac `serverPath`, `appServerPath` (the server this app ships), `startedAtMs`, `pid}`. A remote machine whose server is `older` also has `status.terminalServerUpdate: true`, and after connect_machine or retry_machine `status.terminalServerSessions` (the sessions an update ends; a list leaves it out). Resources are optional self-reported terminal-server observations: `load1` is the 1-minute load average, `cpuCount` logical cores; memory and disk are bytes, memory used is total minus available, disk is the volume holding the server workspace directory labelled by `diskPath`, and `sampledAt` is Unix epoch milliseconds on the server. A call reuses a sample under 30 seconds old or takes one one-shot sample without subscribing. An older server has `machineStatsSupported: false` and no resource fields: unknown (update the terminal server). Disconnected rows omit these fields. Samples are local runtime state, never synced or a reservation; re-check before depending on free disk. The visible Machines tab receives a 30-second stream through the machineStats LiveHub topic; it stops watching when hidden. No secrets are returned. A dashboard's codesense.machines() returns the same list for the page's team."
    )]
    async fn list_machines(
        &self,
        Parameters(params): Parameters<ListMachinesParams>,
    ) -> Result<CallToolResult, ErrorData> {
        let result = commands::machines::list_machines(
            self.app_handle.clone(),
            params.org_slug,
            params.team,
            Some(true),
        )
        .await
        .map_err(|error| invalid_err("list_machines", error))?;
        ok_json(serde_json::json!(result))
    }

